  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 253 PM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nStrong surface high pressure remains in place across the region. \nThe high's center is expected to pass to our north tonight, from \nwest to east. This will cause winds to become more easterly tonight \nand eventually southeasterly on Tuesday. With an added southerly \ncomponent to the wind field, warm air advection looks to commence. A \ngradual warming trend appears likely beginning tomorrow. High \ntemperatures may climb into the 80s by Wednesday.\n\nA broad trough skirts to our north later Wednesday into Thursday \nwith an associated surface low over the Hudson Bay. Though displaced \nwell to our north, this low will drag a surface cold front through \nthe Ohio River Valley. Cold air advection takes hold on Thursday \nwith a cool-down anticipated. Highs dip back into the low 70s both \nThursday and Friday; lows once again into the 40s. Patchy fog is \npossible during the overnights with efficient radiational cooling.\n\nThe pattern repeats with surface high pressure arriving and then \npassing to our northeast on Friday. Winds regain a southeasterly \ncomponent allowing gradual warming to begin again. Highs climbing \ninto the upper 70s to near 80 are possible this weekend. Clear skies \naside from occasional cirrus are expected through Saturday.\n\nDry conditions combined with daytime relative humidity values near \n30 percent may allow for increased fire weather potential through \nSaturday. Surface wind speeds are likely to remain light, thus \nmitigating the threat.\n\nSUNDAY ONWARD\n\nWarming will continue into Sunday depending on whether a disturbance \nover the Gulf lifts north or northeastward. Guidance is beginning to \nhone in on the idea of tropical development over the southwestern \nGulf and taking the resulting system northward in conjunction with a \ncut off upper-level low over Texas. This is a highly uncertain \nsituation, since multiple factors are in play that have yet to \ndevelop.\n\nFirst, the tropical cyclone may not develop at all and simply remain \na tropical wave / moisture plume. Second, the upper-level low over \nTexas fails to materialize or is too fast/slow. Any combination of \nthese may dramatically affect the outcome for Indiana. \n\nWe can boil it down into two scenarios, however. One where it rains \nand one where it does not. Should moisture lift northward into \nIndiana, whether associated with a weak or strong low, then \ntemperatures are likely to be suppressed and rainfall chances high. \nShould the system fail to develop or miss entirely, then above-\naverage warmth continues into the new week. These systems are within \na larger-scale ridge, so avoiding any precipitation will lead to a \ncontinued warming trend.",
